# Markdown Image Handling & Configuration

How the WYSIWYG editor stores images for plain `.md` files, and how the user chooses where they go — simply, mid-edit, per project or globally.

## The problem

In `.hd` / `.hd2`, every document has an injected `id`, so pasted images live in an `id`-keyed folder (`.hd/<id>/`) and are referenced by bare filename. A `.md` file has **no id and no injected frontmatter** (the editor is a guest — see [MD-FORMAT.hd2](MD-FORMAT.hd2)), so that mechanism is unavailable. An image in Markdown is just a **relative path written into the source**:

```markdown
![diagram](guide.assets/diagram.png)
```

That path has to be chosen, the file written to disk, and the link computed relative to the document. The open question is *which folder* — and the answer must avoid colliding with folders a project already uses.

## Conventions in the wild

| Tool | Convention | Type |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Typora | `./${filename}.assets/` — a sibling folder named after the doc | Per-document |
| Obsidian | Configurable: subfolder next to the note, or a central `attachments/` | Per-doc or central |
| Hugo | Page bundles — `my-post/index.md` with images in the same `my-post/` folder | Co-located |
| Gatsby (gatsby-remark-images) | Images co-located next to the `.md`, referenced relatively | Co-located |
| Docusaurus | `static/img/` (served from `/img/`) or co-located | Central or co-located |
| Jekyll | `/assets/` or `/images/` at the site root | Central |

Two families: **per-document / co-located** (self-contained) and **central shared folder** (`assets/`, `images/`, `static/`).

## Why the default should be a per-document folder

The central conventions — `assets/`, `images/`, `static/`, `public/` — are exactly the folders a website project **already uses**, with build-pipeline meaning. Dropping editor images into them pollutes or collides. That is the risk to design against.

A **per-document folder named after the file** (Typora's `${filename}.assets/`) avoids it entirely:

- **No collision** — it's namespaced by the document's own name, so it can't clash with a project-wide `assets/` or `images/`.
- **Self-contained** — one folder holds exactly one document's images; easy to see, move, or delete.
- **Renames cleanly** — the folder moves with the document (it's the direct analog of HD keying by `id`, except keyed by filename since there is no id).
- **Signals intent** — the `.assets` suffix reads as "this document's managed images."

**Recommended default: `${name}.assets/`.** But because the right layout genuinely depends on the host project (a Hugo site wants co-location; a Docusaurus site wants `static/img`), it must be **overridable** — which is what the rest of this document is about.

## How a folder is resolved

The configured value is a **template** that resolves to a target folder, from which the Markdown link is computed:

1. Expand the template's tokens for the current document.
2. If the result is relative, resolve it **against the document's own folder**; a `${workspaceFolder}` token anchors it at the project root instead.
3. Save the pasted image into that folder.
4. Write the image link as a **path relative to the document**, so the Markdown stays portable.

Example — active document `docs/guide.md`, template `${name}.assets`:
→ target folder `docs/guide.assets/` → link `![](guide.assets/image-1.png)`.

## Tokens

A **token** is a placeholder written `${…}` that the editor substitutes with a real value when it resolves the path. It lets one setting be correct for every document instead of a hardcoded path that only fits one file. (This is the same mechanism VS Code uses in `tasks.json` — `${workspaceFolder}`, `${fileBasenameNoExtension}` — and that Typora uses with `${filename}`.)

The supported set is deliberately small:

| Token | Means | For `docs/guide.md` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `${name}` | Document filename without extension | `guide` |
| `${dir}` | The document's containing folder | `docs` |
| `${workspaceFolder}` | The project root | (repo root) |

So `${name}.assets` → `guide.assets`, and `${workspaceFolder}/static/img` points at a central build folder regardless of where the document sits.

## Preset patterns

The value is a template, so one setting expresses every convention — the user just changes the string. These are the presets the helper offers (examples for `docs/guide.md`):

| Preset | Template | Images go to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Folder named after the document (recommended) | `${name}.assets` | `docs/guide.assets/` |
| Next to the document | `.` | `docs/` |
| Shared `assets/` folder | `assets` | `docs/assets/` |
| Shared `images/` folder | `images` | `docs/images/` |
| Central project media folder | `${workspaceFolder}/static/img` | `static/img/` |
| Custom… | (user-edited) | (live preview) |

## Configuration: project-level and system-wide

The convention is a normal VS Code setting the extension declares (e.g. `hd.markdown.assetFolder`, a string, default `${name}.assets`). VS Code's built-in settings hierarchy gives both scopes for free:

- **Project (Workspace)** — written to the project's `.vscode/settings.json`. Committable, so the whole team shares one convention. **This is the recommended scope**, and the one to lead with, since image layout is a property of the repository.
- **System-wide (User)** — written to the user's global settings; a personal default that applies to every project that doesn't override it.

**Precedence:** Workspace overrides User overrides the built-in default. So a user can set a personal default and still let individual projects override it. The extension reads the effective value per-document (`getConfiguration('hd.markdown', documentUri).get('assetFolder')`), so VS Code resolves the right scope automatically.

## The in-editor helper (the important part)

Users will hit this mid-edit — while pasting an image — so it must be **discoverable and concept-first**: they pick a plain-language layout, not a template string. Template editing exists, but only for those who want something custom.

### The picker command

A command — **"HD: Set Where Pasted Images Go"** — reachable from the command palette, a status-bar item, and the editor toolbar. It runs a two-step flow:

**Step 1 — pick the layout.** A quick-pick lists the presets in plain language, each showing a **live preview computed for the current document** and, as secondary detail, the template it maps to:

```
Where should pasted images be saved?

  📁  A folder named after the document        docs/guide.assets/     recommended · ${name}.assets
  📄  Next to the document                      docs/                  · .
  🗂  A shared "assets" folder                  docs/assets/           · assets
  🗂  A shared "images" folder                  docs/images/           · images
  🏗  A central project media folder            static/img/            · ${workspaceFolder}/static/img
  ⚙️  Custom pattern…
```

Because each row shows the actual folder for *this* document, the user chooses by seeing the result — they never have to reason about tokens.

**Step 2 — pick the scope.** A second quick-pick:

```
Apply this to…

  ●  This project (recommended)     saved in .vscode/settings.json, shared with your team
  ○  All my projects                your personal default
```

The extension then writes the value with `config.update(key, value, target)` using the chosen target (Workspace or Global) and confirms: *"Pasted images will go to `docs/guide.assets/` — this project."*

**Custom pattern.** Choosing *Custom…* opens an input box **pre-filled with the current template**, with the token list shown as a hint and a **live-resolved preview** updating as they type (and validation that the result stays inside the workspace). This is the escape hatch — everyone else never sees a token.

### Discoverability

- **Status-bar item** — while a Markdown/hd2 document is active, a small item like `🖼 guide.assets` shows where images currently go; clicking it opens the picker. This is the always-present, mid-edit entry point.
- **First-paste nudge** — the first time an image is pasted in a workspace that hasn't set the option, save to the default and show a non-blocking toast: *"Saved to `guide.assets/`. Change where images go?"* with **[Configure]** / **[Keep]**. It teaches the setting exactly when it's relevant, without blocking the paste.
- **Command palette** — for deliberate access.

## Rename & move

Because links are real relative paths (not id-indirected), renaming or moving a document must **rewrite its image references**, or rely on a per-document folder that moves with it. The `${name}.assets` default plus the editor's existing rename hook (which already renames HD asset folders) is the least-fragile combination; central-folder layouts push more of this burden onto the user.

## Publishing caveat

If the `.md` is served by a static site, the resolved relative path must exist in the build output. Per-document and co-located layouts are safe for page-bundle generators (Hugo, Gatsby) that process the whole document folder; central layouts must point at a directory the site actually publishes (e.g. `static/`). This host-dependence is the reason the convention is a setting rather than a fixed rule — and the reason the default is the safe, isolated per-document folder.

## Open sub-decisions

- Exact setting key and default (`hd.markdown.assetFolder` = `${name}.assets`?).
- Whether the status-bar item is on by default or opt-in.
- Whether the first-paste nudge appears once per workspace, or once per user.
- Filename scheme inside the folder (`image-1.png` incrementing, as HD does today, vs. a hash or the source filename).
- Whether to auto-detect the host generator (Hugo/Astro/Docusaurus config present) and pre-select a matching preset.
